Implement a specific task or phase from a plan file and keep the plan in sync.

Arguments:
- Accept a plan file path plus either a task id, a task title, or a phase name.
- If the user gives only a phase, implement all pending tasks that belong to that phase.
- If the target is ambiguous, resolve it from the plan contents before editing code.

Operating rules:
1. Read the target plan file first.
2. Find the requested task or phase in the `todos` list and in the body of the plan.
3. Read the relevant project code and architecture before proposing changes.
4. In Unity projects, default to script/text changes only unless the user explicitly asks for scene, prefab, or other Unity asset edits.
5. Treat `.unity`, `.prefab`, `.asset`, `.mat`, `.anim`, `.controller`, and `.meta` edits as Unity-side changes unless the user explicitly authorizes them.
6. Identify prerequisites that are required from the user, from Unity Editor, or from external assets/configuration.
7. If hard prerequisites are missing and implementation cannot proceed safely:
   - Do not mark the task done.
   - Stop and output a `Prerequisites Needed From User` section listing exactly what is needed.
   - Also output a `Blocked Reason` section.
8. If implementation can proceed:
   - Implement the task fully in code and files.
   - If Unity Editor actions are required or the requested task needs scene/prefab wiring, make all code-side changes first and then list the exact Unity actions needed.
   - If the user did not explicitly allow Unity asset edits, do not edit scene/prefab/asset files even if you could. Leave those as `Post-Implementation Requirements`.
   - Keep changes aligned with `.cursor/rules/general-implementation-rules.mdc`.
9. After implementation:
   - Update the plan file.
   - Mark the implemented task or tasks as `completed` only when the repository-side work for that task is actually finished without requiring still-pending Unity asset edits.
   - If only part of a broader phase was completed, mark only the finished todos as `completed`.
   - If only supporting script groundwork was completed for a task that still needs Unity-side wiring, keep that task pending and record the groundwork in `## Progress Updates`.
   - Add a short implementation note under a `## Progress Updates` section at the end of the plan if one does not exist.
   - Each progress entry must include:
     - date
     - completed task ids
     - short summary of what was implemented
     - remaining follow-ups or Unity-side wiring if any
10. Never mark a task `completed` unless the implementation is actually finished in the repository for the code side of the work.
11. If tests or validation are relevant, run them when possible and report the results.

Required output format:

## Target
- Plan: `<path>`
- Task or Phase: `<resolved target>`

## Prerequisites Needed From User
- Write `None` if nothing is required before implementation.
- Otherwise list the exact prerequisites.

## Implementation
- Summarize what you changed.
- Mention important files touched.

## Plan Update
- List which todo ids were marked `completed`.
- List any todo ids left pending.

## Post-Implementation Requirements
- Write `None` if there is nothing the user needs to do after implementation.
- Otherwise list required Unity Editor wiring, asset creation, inspector assignments, testing steps, or external setup still needed.

## Validation
- Report tests run, checks performed, or why validation could not be completed.

Behavior details:
- Prefer implementing one concrete task cleanly rather than partially touching many tasks.
- If the user asks for a full phase and it is too large to complete safely in one pass, implement as much as is completeable end-to-end, mark only finished todos as completed, and clearly list the remaining pending ones.
- If the requested work requires scene or prefab changes that cannot be performed fully in code, still implement all supporting runtime code and then list exact Unity steps under `Post-Implementation Requirements`.
- If the user has not explicitly authorized Unity asset edits, never perform those edits as part of this command. Treat them as follow-up requirements.
- When updating the plan file, preserve existing structure and metadata.
- Do not invent new task ids unless you are explicitly asked to rewrite the plan.

Plan progress entry template:

## Progress Updates

### YYYY-MM-DD - <task-id or phase-name>
- Completed: `<todo-id-1>`, `<todo-id-2>`
- Summary: `<what was implemented>`
- Follow-ups: `<remaining Unity/editor/manual requirements or "None">`

Goal:
- This command should be usable repeatedly for each task or phase in `META_FEATURES_INTEGRATION_PLAN.md` or any other plan file, with the plan always reflecting what has actually been implemented.
